WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.280 --> 00:00:02.929
Grabbing things in space is tricky.

00:00:02.929 --> 00:00:06.360
Unless things are designed to fit together…

00:00:06.360 --> 00:00:09.080
...it can a bit hard to latch on.

00:00:09.080 --> 00:00:11.840
With no atmosphere, suction is out.

00:00:11.840 --> 00:00:14.020
And sticky adhesives are rendered useless

00:00:14.020 --> 00:00:17.120
by extreme cold and degrading radiation.

00:00:17.440 --> 00:00:19.960
So scientists looked to nature for ideas,

00:00:19.960 --> 00:00:23.100
finding inspiration in the toes of geckos

00:00:23.400 --> 00:00:26.260
-- who easily scramble up smooth surfaces.

00:00:26.260 --> 00:00:28.320
The key to this remarkable ability are

00:00:28.320 --> 00:00:30.320
microscopic hairs strategically arranged

00:00:30.320 --> 00:00:32.180
on a gecko’s feet.

00:00:32.180 --> 00:00:35.360
When pressed, these hairs flatten out against a surface,

00:00:35.580 --> 00:00:38.080
and tiny forces of molecular attraction

00:00:38.080 --> 00:00:40.180
add up across the may points of contact -

00:00:40.180 --> 00:00:42.180
- creating instant grip.

00:00:42.600 --> 00:00:44.300
Scientists tried to mimic this technique

00:00:44.300 --> 00:00:45.840
by creating tiny wedges

00:00:45.840 --> 00:00:47.740
that act similarly to these hairs.

00:00:48.300 --> 00:00:50.160
Forces that smoosh the wedges

00:00:50.160 --> 00:00:52.040
into a surface create grip,

00:00:52.360 --> 00:00:54.980
while tangential forces peel the material away.

00:00:55.240 --> 00:00:58.080
By adding this material to robotic contraptions,

00:00:58.080 --> 00:01:01.120
scientists could pick up and release objects remotely...

00:01:01.460 --> 00:01:03.940
...and even direct a robot to walk across a wall.

00:01:03.940 --> 00:01:05.460
Scientists tested the grippers

00:01:05.460 --> 00:01:07.460
on a minimal friction platform

00:01:07.460 --> 00:01:09.760
to show that robots in zero gravity

00:01:09.760 --> 00:01:12.940
should be able to grasp objects like solar panels

00:01:12.940 --> 00:01:15.400
instead of simply pushing them away.

00:01:15.600 --> 00:01:18.240
And during the nose-dive induced weightlessness

00:01:18.280 --> 00:01:21.060
of NASA’s famous zero gravity aircraft,

00:01:21.060 --> 00:01:24.100
training astronauts used them to play catch

00:01:24.100 --> 00:01:26.100
with both flat and curved objects.

00:01:26.460 --> 00:01:29.500
The researchers also sent miniature grippers into space

00:01:29.500 --> 00:01:32.040
where they stuck to the walls of the ISS.

00:01:33.060 --> 00:01:35.060
Developing such a tool could be important

00:01:35.060 --> 00:01:36.560
for many space projects,

00:01:36.660 --> 00:01:38.660
from clearing out floating debris

00:01:38.760 --> 00:01:39.840
orbiting our planet,

00:01:40.100 --> 00:01:42.660
to performing dangerous tasks with robots,

00:01:42.660 --> 00:01:45.040
so that astronauts can stay safe.

